# Accessibility

Not a checklist bolted on before launch. Most of it is free if the markup is right, and expensive to retrofit if it is not.

## Semantics First

The correct element brings keyboard support, focus behavior, and screen-reader semantics with it, for free.

- `<button>` for actions, `<a href>` for navigation. A `div` with `onClick` is unreachable by keyboard, invisible to assistive tech, and does not fire on Enter/Space.
- Real landmarks: `<header> <nav> <main> <aside> <footer>`. One `<main>` per page.
- One `<h1>`, then a heading order with no skipped levels. Screen-reader users navigate by heading — a broken outline is a broken table of contents.
- `<ul>`/`<ol>` for lists, `<table>` with `<th scope>` for tabular data.

**The first rule of ARIA is not to use ARIA.** `role="button"` on a div is strictly worse than a button. ARIA adds semantics; it never adds behavior.

## Keyboard

Every interactive element is reachable and operable with a keyboard alone. Test by unplugging the mouse and completing the primary flow.

- Visible focus indicator, ≥3:1 contrast against its background. Never `outline: none` without a stronger replacement.
- `:focus-visible` so keyboard users get a ring and mouse users do not.
- DOM order matches visual order. Positive `tabindex` values reorder the whole page and are effectively always a bug.
- Modals: focus moves in on open, is trapped inside, ESC closes, focus returns to the trigger on close.
- A "skip to content" link as the first focusable element.

## Forms

- Every input has a `<label for>` or an `aria-label`. A placeholder is not a label — it vanishes on typing and usually fails contrast.
- Errors are associated with `aria-describedby` and announced via a live region. A red border alone communicates nothing to a screen-reader or a colorblind user.
- Group related controls in `<fieldset>` with a `<legend>`.
- Correct `type` and `autocomplete` attributes — this is a large usability win on mobile, not just an a11y one.
- Never validate on every keystroke before first blur; it announces errors while the user is mid-word.

## Visual

- Contrast: 4.5:1 body text, 3:1 for large text and UI component boundaries.
- Never color alone to convey meaning — pair it with an icon, text, or pattern.
- Layout survives 200% zoom and a 320px viewport without horizontal scroll.
- Touch targets ≥44×44px with adequate spacing.
- Respect `prefers-reduced-motion` — vestibular disorders make parallax and large transforms genuinely nauseating.

## Dynamic Content

- Live regions (`aria-live="polite"`) for async status: saved, error, results count.
- Focus management on route change in an SPA — otherwise focus stays on the old page and the user hears nothing.
- Loading states announced, not just visually spinning.
- `alt` text describes purpose: `alt="Delete invoice 4821"`, `alt=""` for decorative images.

## Verify

Automated tooling (axe, Lighthouse) catches roughly a third of issues. It cannot judge whether your alt text is meaningful or your focus order makes sense.

Manual pass: keyboard-only, screen reader on one flow (VoiceOver/NVDA), 200% zoom, and a contrast check.

## NEVER

- `outline: none` with no replacement.
- `aria-hidden` on a focusable element — it creates a control screen-reader users can reach but cannot identify.
- Autoplaying audio or video with sound.
- A time limit with no way to extend it.
- `role` or `tabindex` used to make a `div` behave like a native element.
